66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"- **Ryuji**: The candidates are now visible. Given the staffing gaps, I need to see which projects rely on the missing technicians.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"- **Victoria**: @Ryuji, the spectroscopy suite’s downtime is the deciding factor. Any proposal needing it faces an unacceptable risk of delay.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, the free simulation hours make the computational risk negligible. The real constraint is the animal facility cost, not the spectroscopy downtime.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I agree on the cost shift. But @Shizue’s point about lasting utility is key. Which candidate offers the most durable resource for the field?","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""name_label"",""name"":""Midori"",""self"":true,""raw"":""Midori: @Benjamin, I agree on the cost shift. But @Shizue’s point about lasting utility is key. Which candidate offers the most durable resource for the field?""}]}"
70,3,3,talk,Shizue,,#### Turn 3 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, the spectroscopy suite is critical for C, but the animal facility costs threaten B. We must weigh equipment risk against budget strain.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, exactly. B faces the highest financial strain due to the vivarium rate hikes, while C faces the highest operational risk from the idle spectroscopy suite.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, precisely. B’s biological risk is compounded by the expensive animal units, while C’s computational needs are now free. The financial exposure is clearly on B.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, D’s standardized metadata and rapid repository clearance align perfectly with your preference for lasting utility. It avoids the biological and equipment risks entirely.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I vote for D. Its catalogue nature ensures lasting utility, avoiding the unresolved questions that plagued our past mechanism-focused awards.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I vote for D. It avoids the missing technicians and the seconded analysts, relying instead on stable field infrastructure and available data pipelines.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I vote for D. It sidesteps the idle spectroscopy suite and the expensive animal units, relying instead on stable field sites and rapid data clearance.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I vote for D. It leverages the free repository clearance and avoids the expensive animal units and idle spectroscopy suite, aligning with our financial and operational constraints.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I vote for D. Its durable data resource and rapid repository clearance address my concerns about administrative delays, offering lasting value without the operational risks of the other proposals.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
